The first substantive check is already decisive about scope: Lemma 2.1 constrains Laurent coefficients in the other polynomial’s root parameter, not ordinary coefficients in one shared \(\pi\)-polynomial. On the ray, its permitted first non-\(4\)-divisible exponent is exactly \(M'_2=n'-3\), and \(\Phi\) gives \((\delta'_2,\delta'_1)=(-1,t/(3t+1))\). Thus the Newton data pass even at \(t=1\); Moh’s emptiness occurs only after the approximate-root cancellation equations. I’m treating that as a necessary-screen pass, not as attainment.
