   192	```
   193	
   194	In Moh's `eta=P^{-1/n}` expansion of `Q`, `M_2` is the first exponent whose nonzero
   195	coefficient breaks divisibility by `d_2=4`. The requested datum is realized at the
   196	support level by a nonzero *constant* coefficient at
   197	
   198	```text
   199	M_2=n-3 < n-1.
   200	```
   201	
   202	Lemma 2.1 permits that constant and places the forced quadratic coefficient two
   203	indices later, at `n-1`. Equivalently, in the reciprocal
   204	`tilde_eta=Q^{-1/m}` expansion of `P`, the exponent dictionary gives
   205	
   206	```text
   207	N=M_2-(n-m)=m-3=8t+1,
   208	```
   209	
   210	again a permitted nonzero constant, followed two indices later by the quadratic
   211	anchor at `m-1`.
   212	
   213	**PROVED-HERE.** Consequently the proposed Lemma/Newton contradiction does not occur:
   214	
   215	```text
   216	(n-1)-M_2 = 2,
   217	(m-1)-N   = 2.
   218	```
   219	
   220	The datum is compatible for all `t`, including `t=1`; compatibility is not
   221	attainment.
   222	
   223	### 3.2 `V_2`, top face, and Puiseux separations
   224	
   225	**SOURCE-READ + DERIVED.** Definition 5.1/Proposition 5.3 requires for the sole
   226	`D_2 -> D_1` extension
   227	
   228	```text
   229	V_3*d_2/d_3 >= V_2 > d_2/(n-M_2),  V_3=d_3=1,
   230	4 >= 3 > 4/3.
   231	```
   232	
   233	Thus `V_2=3` is allowed, not forced by Lemma 2.1. Definition 5.1(1) gives the
   234	major-disc root counts
   235	
   236	```text
   237	P: (n/d_2)V_2 = 9t+3,     complement n/4 = 3t+1,
   238	Q: (m/d_2)V_2 = 6t+3,     complement m/4 = 2t+1.
   239	```
   240	
   241	After a linear normalization the common top approximate root is
   242	
   243	```text
   244	h_top=pi^3(pi-a*gamma),  a!=0,
   245	P_top=h_top^(3t+1)=pi^(9t+3)(pi-a*gamma)^(3t+1),
   246	Q_top=h_top^(2t+1)=pi^(6t+3)(pi-a*gamma)^(2t+1).
   247	```
   248	
   249	**DERIVED.** In `(gamma exponent, pi exponent)` coordinates the forced top segments
   250	have endpoints
   251	
   252	```text
   253	NP(P): (0,12t+4) -- (3t+1,9t+3),
   254	NP(Q): (0, 8t+4) -- (2t+1,6t+3).
   255	```
   256	
   257	Both have ordinary edge slope `-1`. The two tangent directions are `pi=0` and
   258	`pi=a gamma`; their cross-disc logarithmic separation is `delta_2'=-1`. The next
   259	major-disc separation `delta_1'` is computed in §4. These segments and separations
   260	do not specify every lower Newton face.
   261	
   262	## 4. Definition 5.1, Phi, Proposition 5.3, and descended (8)-(13)
   263	
   264	### 4.1 Radius calculation
   265	
   266	**SOURCE-READ (Moh p.179).** For a major tower, Definition 5.1(3) says
   267	
   268	```text
   269	delta_i^51 = 1
   270	 - (n-M_i) prod_{j=i+1}^s [V_j(n-M_j)-d_j]
   271	   / ((n-M_s-1) prod_{j=i+1}^s [V_j(n-M_{j-1})-d_j]).
   272	```
   273	
   274	For `s'=2`, the ray data give
   275	
   276	```text
   277	delta_2^51 = -1/2,
   278	delta_1^51 = t/[2(3t+1)].
   279	```
   280	
   281	**DERIVED using the banked Phi rule.** Since `k_jac=1`, conversion to geometric
   282	`ord_{gamma^{-1}}` radii multiplies by `k_jac+1=2`:
   283	
   284	```text
   285	(delta_2',delta_1')=(-1, t/(3t+1)).
   286	```
   287	
   288	Sympy checks include
   289	
   290	```text
   291	t=1: (-1,1/4),
   292	t=2: (-1,2/7).
   293	```
   294	
   295	**SOURCE-READ / DERIVED.** Proposition 5.3 is used nontrivially once: it extends the
   296	base disc `D_2` to `D_1` using the multiplicity `V_2=3` and the displayed window.
   297	There are no intermediate `r>=3` sibling-extension stages when `s'=2`.
   298	
   299	### 4.2 Conditions (8)-(13)
   300	
   301	**DERIVED.** With geometric radii, the lcm of the reduced denominators above
   302	`delta_1'` is `1`, so condition (8) gives
   303	
   304	```text
   305	A_1=denominator(delta_1')=3t+1=e.
   306	```
   307	
   308	Conditions (9)-(11) have an empty intermediate index range. The bottom alternative
   309	(12) holds identically:
   310	
   311	```text
   312	A_1 | (n/d_2)V_2       because 3e,
   313	A_1 | (m/d_2)V_2 - 1   because 3(2t+1)-1=2e.
   314	```
   315	
   316	Alternative (13) fails for every `t>=1` because its two numbers are `2e+1` and
   317	`3e-1`. Thus the descended (8)-(13) were explicitly evaluated and used as a
   318	**compatibility check**; they give no obstruction. Phi was used both here (to obtain
   319	`A_1`) and in the `t=2` order truncation below.
   320	
   321	## 5. The `t=2` approximate-root truncation
   322	
   323	### 5.1 The source-correct order bound
   324	
   325	Set `(n,m;M_2,V_2)=(28,20;25,3)` and `delta_1'=2/7`.
   326	
   327	**SOURCE-READ (Moh p.149, Theorem 1.2).** If a coherent complete system has accuracy
   328	`lambda` and `h` is its quasi-approximate root of exponent `r`, the coefficient at
   329	`h`-deficit `i` has order at every coherent root at least `(lambda/r)i`.
   330	
   331	**DERIVED.** For `P`, the two coherent-root multiplicities are `(21,7)`, so
   332	
   333	```text
   334	lambda_P = 21*(2/7)+7*(-1) = -1,
   335	lambda_P/7 = -1/7.
   336	```
   337	
   338	For `Q`, they are `(15,5)`, so
   339	
   340	```text
   341	lambda_Q = 15*(2/7)+5*(-1) = -5/7,
   342	lambda_Q/5 = -1/7.
   343	```
   344	
   345	Hence both coefficient families obey `ord C_i >= -i/7`. Using
   346	`-i*delta_1'=-2i/7` would be a weaker necessary superset; it is not the source's
   347	sharp Theorem-1.2 truncation. At `t=1` the two values coincide, which explains why
   348	this distinction is easy to miss.
   349	
   350	### 5.2 A 30-variable necessary chart
   351	
   352	Normalize the nonzero top-line coefficient and define
   353	
   354	```text
   355	z=pi-gamma,
   356	B=pi*z+b1*pi+b2,
   357	A=pi*B+b3,
   358	h=pi*A+b4=pi^4-gamma*pi^3+b1*pi^3+b2*pi^2+b3*pi+b4.
   359	```
   360	
   361	**DERIVED / PROVED-HERE.** Intersecting the order bound at the major and minor
   362	coherent roots gives the following coefficient spaces:
   363	
   364	At the major root,
   365	`ord(1,A,B,z,gamma)=(0,-3/7,-5/7,-1,-1)`; at the normalized minor root,
   366	`1,z` have nonnegative order and `A,B` have positive order. Comparing these values
   367	with `-i/7` gives the table without dividing by any parameter-dependent leader.
   368	
   369	| deficit `i` | basis | dimension |
   370	|---:|---|---:|
   371	| 1 | `1` | 1 |
   372	| 2 | `1` | 1 |
   373	| 3 | `1,A` | 2 |
   374	| 4 | `1,A` | 2 |
   375	| 5 | `1,A,B` | 3 |
   376	| 6 | `1,A,B` | 3 |
   377	| 7 | `1,gamma,A,B,z` | 5 |
   378	
   379	Write
   380	
   381	```text
   382	P=h^7 + sum_{i=1}^7 alpha_i h^(7-i),
   383	Q=h^5 + sum_{i=2}^5 beta_i h^(5-i).
   384	```
   385	
   386	The missing `h^4` term of `Q` is the Tschirnhausen/approximate-root normalization.
   387	The table gives `17` alpha parameters and `1+2+2+3=8` beta parameters. With four
   388	`b_i` and the Jacobian scalar `c`, this necessary-chart upper-bound count is
   389	
   390	```text
   391	4 + 17 + 8 + 1 = 30 unknowns.
   392	```
   393	
   394	**PROVED-HERE (safe gauges).** First subtract the scalar part of `beta_5` from `Q`.
   395	Next, since `alpha_2` is a scalar, replace `P` by `P-alpha_2 Q`; the induced changes
   396	`alpha_4-alpha_2 beta_2`, ..., `alpha_7-alpha_2 beta_5` remain in their displayed
   397	filtration spaces. Finally subtract the scalar part of the new `alpha_7` from `P`.
   398	This invertible triangular target change has determinant one, preserves `J(Q,P)`,
   399	monicity, degrees and the two-disc filtration, and reduces the solver chart to
   400	
   401	```text
   402	26 polynomial parameters + c = 27 unknowns.
   403	```
   404	
   405	In the reciprocal Laurent expansion, the `P-alpha_2 Q` change starts at exponent
   406	`-m=-20`, divisible by `4`, so it cannot manufacture an earlier non-`4`-divisible
   407	anchor term. The two constant shifts are invisible to the infinity characteristic
   408	data.
   409	
   410	**DERIVED (uniform count).** For general ray parameter `t`, Theorem 1.2 gives the
   411	common bound `ord C_i>=-i/(3t+1)`. The both-disc dimensions are `1` through `i=t`,
   412	`2` through `2t`, `3` through `3t`, and `5` at `i=3t+1`. Hence the ungauged
   413	coefficient count including `c` is `9t+12`. This is linear growth, not a uniform
   414	obstruction.
   415	
   416	### 5.3 Exact Jacobian equations
   417	
   418	**PROVED-HERE.** For terms `a h^r,b h^s`, the driver uses the identity
   419	
   420	```text
   421	J(a h^r,b h^s)
   422	 = h^(r+s)J(a,b)
   423	 + h^(r+s-1)[s b J(a,h)+r a J(h,b)].
   424	```
   425	
   426	It sums all pairs, then Euclidean-divides each coefficient by the monic polynomial
   427	`h` in `pi`, carrying the quotient to the next `h`-power. No leading coefficient is
   428	divided out and no zero-leader branch is discarded. Equating the four remainder
   429	coordinates to `c gamma` gives, **MEASURED**:
   430	
   431	```text
   432	37 scalar equations at h-levels 0..9,
   433	parameter degrees: 1:1, 2:6, 3:16, 4:14,
   434	6395 bytes of generator text.
   435	```
   436	
   437	The driver uses `J(Q,P)=c gamma`; the requested convention `J(P,Q)=c gamma` differs
   438	only by replacing the unrestricted nonzero scalar `c` with `-c`.
   439	
   440	The declared Singular ring is over `Q`. For the decisive gauged run, generator order
   441	is exactly the 26 surviving chart parameters, `c`, then the Rabinowitsch variable
   442	`T`, with degree-reverse-lexicographic monomial order. The main ideal is
   443	
   444	```text
   445	I=<37 coefficient equations, T*c-1>.
   446	```
   447	
   448	The top-line coefficient is already normalized to `-1`; `T*c-1` selects the required
   449	nonzero-Jacobian component. The still-untranslated tuple-level Laurent coefficient is
   450	discussed next; it is not the Lemma anchor and is not silently called `c`.
   451	
   452	## 6. Saturation, controls, and bounded result
   453	
   454	### 6.1 Wrapper and semantic controls
   455	
   456	**MEASURED.** Before the main standard basis, the generated Singular program runs in
   457	the identical declared ring:
   458	
   459	```text
   460	EMPTY wrapper:    <c, T*c-1>       -> reduce(1,G)=0       PASS
   461	NONEMPTY wrapper: <c-1, T*c-1>     -> reduce(1,G)!=0      PASS
   462	```
   463	
   464	The independent actual-pair control `(pi,pi-gamma^2/2)` is monic in `pi`, passes the
